  "evidence": "TEXT_C: Cancel Trident replacement; end arms export promotion; humane immigration system and end detention centres; legalise cannabis with regulated sales plus heroin‑assisted treatment and safe consumption rooms; shift from short prison sentences to restorative justice; proportional representation, elected second chamber, citizens’ assemblies and a People’s Constitution; phased Universal Basic Income and a 10:1 pay‑ratio cap; cancel major road building, airport expansions and HS2; no new nuclear; moratorium on GM. TEXT_D: Maintain a minimum nuclear deterrent and meet NATO 2% defence spending; £1bn for community policing and a public‑health approach to serious violence; +1p on Income Tax moving toward a dedicated Health and Care Tax; electoral reform to STV and Lords reform within the existing constitutional framework; decriminalise possession with a regulated cannabis market (more limited than wholesale decriminalisation); end the hostile environment and cap detention at 28 days rather than abolishing detention; corporate governance and executive‑pay accountability; net‑zero by 2045 with substantial but measured infrastructure investment.",
  "reasoning": "With Authority and Loyalty weighted highest, TEXT_D better preserves and funds core national and security institutions (nuclear deterrent, NATO, policing) and pursues incremental constitutional and justice changes, whereas TEXT_C abolishes Trident, ends arms export promotion, proposes sweeping constitutional redesign, expansive decriminalisation, and ending detention—moves more likely to trigger authority‑subversion concerns. On Equality/Proportionality and Care both propose strong measures, but TEXT_D’s fiscally bounded approach (hypothecated tax, corporate accountability) better fits proportionality while still addressing care, aligning more closely with the overall profile.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_D"
}
